hi there steve kaufman here again i  haven't done a video in quite a while um  i thought i would report back on my  trip to europe and uh you may remember  that uh one of the main  goals was to spend some time in prague  and i had announced my strategy of five  days to fluency  the idea being that i would set myself  this goal of uh going to where the  language was spoken i was studying czech  and i would prepare for it and i would  build up to it and that i would have  this tremendous passive knowledge of the  language and when i got there  within five days following on the advice  of dr hooligan off within three days my  passive knowledge would become active  and uh  so what was the result  well i would say that after five days in  prague i did not become fluent in  check  however  i consider the whole experiment to be  tremendously successful  it's successful because of the way it's  structured i set myself a goal i'm going  to go somewhere in a year  i study towards that goal as i come  closer to the data i study even harder  and when i go there and and this was my  experience in prague i was able to  understand everything i felt comfortable  i was able to talk to people i had it  set up so that i was speaking seven six  seven eight hours a day of check  and certainly uh my check in improved  tremendously  my comprehension improved my my ability  to read i'm reading novels now with more  comfort  than i had before the whole czech  language thing has become more real to  me  uh the final evening in prague i had a  an evening with a couple that invited me  to dinner and we spoke entirely in check  and i was quite comfortable i could  understand everything they were saying i  was able to get my meaning across  so all of that was good  however i don't think i achieved fluency  um  i still stumble  and i did a sort of a  video with yarda but unfortunately the  sound is so low that i can't use it  and so what i'm going to do is perhaps  next week i'm going to have a discussion  with a tutor maybe yarda maybe someone  else and try to record it so you can see  just exactly what the state of my check  is  um  unfortunately after my it's actually  four days in prague  i then had not unfortunately but  unfortunately for my check  i had  three weeks  in portugal spain and then three days in  berlin so essentially  for almost  a month now i have not been using my  check  and on top of that i've decided that i  should really do something about my  korean  so i have now this week started up  studying korean again  so  i'm trying to maintain my check  i try to read something or listen to  something every day and check i'm still  trying to maintain my russian because  the influence of check on my russian was  to kind  of uh caused me to stumble a lot more  when speaking russian  and of course when i was in germany i  was so motivated to improve my german  that i figured i should do more with  german because we had a  meet-up with our  members in berlin and i i guess i really  had trouble  expressing myself as well as i would  like to express myself in german  and so i think my german has fallen  quite a bit  so going forward here i'm going to be  trying to really focus on korean with  the idea of going to korea and doing  another five days to fluency  test just to see how far i can push  myself with this objective of going  there  five days is not enough so hopefully  i'll get myself a little longer  uh but at the main in the meantime i  also want to maintain my uh check in  russian and german so we'll see how it  goes  um  one other thing i should report back on  was this conference at berlin called  spochan  they translated that as language and  business  i went there in the hope that i could  interest someone there  in link  you know  corporate language training people  unfortunately the conference was was  well attended by teachers and coaches  and trainers and academics  but there was there were hardly any  representatives there  from corporations in other words the  potential clients for these people  offering these language training  services  so that was a bit of a disappointment  there were a number of presentations  some were better than others  number of themes that would come up  quite uh quite often one was this idea  this this concern about intercultural  communication because more and more  companies are hiring people of different  language backgrounds and within europe  you have different working groups within  multinational corporations where people  have to work across language and  cultural barriers and so there's a  concern that people need to be trained  in in how to deal with people of  different language and cultural  backgrounds even though everyone is  speaking english typically  i am a little bit skeptical about how  much you can teach  these cultural skills  uh in my experience in japan i have  found that people who are sincere  and  respected other people  and  performed  in terms of their business dealings they  managed to do well  and that these sort of pre-packaged  stereotypes about different cultures  were of limited use the number of useful  bits of advice  is quite limited  and there is a danger that you treat  people as sort of simply tokens of some  culture and therefore people who must  conform to a certain stereotype rather  than dealing with them as individuals  personally i think a better way to  understand people of different cultures  is to try to learn their language  and learn about their history and stuff  and these are things we have to do on  our own i'm not  quite sure how effective these uh  cultural trainers are  another thing that came up at the  conference was this idea of english as a  lingua franca the idea being there that  since  a majority of english speakers are not  native speakers we should be teaching  english differently  than we teach other languages that the  native speaker is not necessarily the  model and also that in a group where  people speak english at different levels  of competence first of all the native  speaker has a big advantage in these  multinational corporations but also  people who speak english well have an  advantage over people who don't speak  well so we should learn how to  communicate  in a way that makes everybody happy and  comfortable again maybe it's a nice idea  but i don't know how practical that is  obviously the better you speak the  language the greater advantage you're  going to have i don't think it's  necessarily a good idea to talk down to  people  i'm just going to see  yeah just hold on a sec here  hello  hi  hi  i yeah i'm in the middle of doing a  video right now  can i call you in 10 minutes  okay  bye  yeah rather than starting all over again  so yeah english is a  language lingua franca is kind of a new  way of teaching english  uh  you know they gave some examples that  you know in britain if they say you know  we're on a sticky wicket that people  don't know what that means but those  things are not that common i think most  people learning a language  even though the teacher may not be a  native speaker and needn't be a native  speaker the model of the language is  typically the language as spoken by a  native speaker  and we can decide whether we prefer to  learn  the french that's spoken in quebec or in  southern france or in i don't know north  africa or paris  uh or the english is spoken in australia  or spoken in south africa or india  whichever we find the most useful but  we're still going to imitate native  speakers basically so i think the model  is still a native speaker and i'm not  sure that teaching people to talk down  to other people is all that useful maybe  i'm a bit cynical here but i didn't see  that as all that useful  uh some of the other themes there were  someone suggested that really the skills  that are required now are either very  low skills for the hospitality uh  industry like uh  a1a2 on the european scale or very  advanced language skills for  professionals  and my feeling there again was that i  wouldn't want to deal with a hotel clerk  who only had a1 or a2 in in english i  think  as we learned we just continued to  improve and i think most people are  going to end up in the in the b in the  sort of intermediate category  and um  i'm also of the mind that training  people for specific  uh  jobs and the specific job vocabulary  is not always the best policy i think  it's better to give people an overall  grounding in the language and then let  them focus on things that are of of  interest to them where the you know the  vocabulary is relevant but you still  need a pretty sound basis in the  language so  um  yeah so i guess i had the impression  that that um yeah one other thing that  came up too was this idea that it's not  what you know but what it's what you can  do in the language that matters and so  again there were many examples of  teachers explaining what they did to get  people to use the language  the idea that you have to use it or you  don't have it and and as some of you may  know i really think there's tremendous  value in acquiring  a passive  capability in the language this the  ability to understand vocabulary that  that builds up your potential it gives  you a strong base rather than trying at  an early stage to to say a few things  uh which may only trigger a fluent  response from someone else and then  you're hopelessly lost  uh so uh you know i came away from the  conference thinking that it's  understandable if you're running a  classroom that you need to have certain  activities and that the goal is going to  try to get people to to say things and  do things and you're going to try to  target  uh specific you know maybe job-related  subject matter and have role-playing and  stuff like that but  for me as an independent learner someone  who learns for fun  i'll stick with the way i like to learn  but you know nothing wrong with the way  other people like to learn and that's  why getting back to my prague experience  i didn't become fluent but i consider  the whole thing a success because i feel  tremendously satisfied at what i am able  to do in czech or in the other languages  and i think that's the important thing  however we choose to learn whatever we  like to do  we should regularly give ourselves  credit for what we have achieved and we  should do the things that we like to do  because that ensures that we stay with  it  and if we stay with it  uh and if you have a good attitude and  if we learn to notice the language those  are the big three keys from  that lady at san diego state university  we are going to do well  we're going to do well not only by our  own standards but by the standards of  other people  so there you have it just a brief  summary of my trip to europe and i hope  to start you know getting back to doing  these videos more regularly thanks for  listening bye for now

